 your faculty advisor's unique CRN."}]},{"id":"GGIS 391","subject":"GGIS","number":"391","title":"Honors Independent Study","creditHours":"2 TO 4 hours","minHours":2,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Prerequisite: Junior standing; consent of honors adviser.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"74217","code":"","typeCode":"IND","group":"ind","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Independent Study","typeCode":"IND","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This CRN is not valid for registration. Please complete a <a href=\"https://forms.illinois.edu/sec/1526888411\">GGIS 391 Independent Study webform</a> to get your faculty advisor's unique CRN."}]},{"id":"GGIS 392","subject":"GGIS","number":"392","title":"Geography & GIS Internship","creditHours":"0 TO 3 hours","minHours":0,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Approved for Letter and S/U grading. May be repeated in separate terms up to 6 hours. Prerequisite: Consent of faculty sponsor and Director of Undergraduate Studies; at least two courses taken within Geography & GIS.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"74218","code":"","typeCode":"IND","group":"ind","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Independent Study","typeCode":"IND","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":null}]},{"id":"GGIS 395","subject":"GGIS","number":"395","title":"Topics in Geography & GIS","creditHours":"3 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"May be repeated in the same term up to 6 hours if topics vary or in separate terms up to 9 hours if topics vary. Prerequisite: Restricted to Undergraduates.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"79085","code":"JM","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T"],"start":840,"end":1010,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["McAlister, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This course explores the historical and material landscapes of the United States. Students will learn about regional architecture styles and spread, historical and cultural influences, and the various impacts that geography has had on the built environment of the country. Key topics include migration patterns, observing the built landscape, and historic preservation. Students will develop the skills needed to understand and work with the National Register of Historic Places and state and local historic preservation offices."},{"crn":"78853","code":"SZ","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["M","W"],"start":630,"end":710,"location":"2084 Natural History Building","instructors":["Zaragocin, S"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"This course examines the dialectical relationship between gender and the social construction of space. Students will engage with key themes in feminist geography, including gender, space and place, embodiment and everyday life, and intersectionality. Moving across multiple scales—from the body to the world system—we will explore how gender, sexuality, race, class, and coloniality shape spatial relations in diverse contexts. The course draws on feminist geographic scholarship from both the Global North and the Global South, emphasizing how feminist methodologies—such as reflexivity, translation, participatory research, social cartography, and embodied methods—can produce more equitable and transformat